The database should use foreign keys, indexes, unique constraints and appropriate deletion rules.

For example:
- A member number must be unique.
- An event slug must be unique.
- An email should not be assumed unique if the church permits family members to share email addresses.
- A prayer request must remain associated with its follow-up history.
- Removing a fellowship must not automatically delete member records.
- Deleting media must not leave broken references across the website.

31. User roles and permissions
Do not create one admin account where everyone has unrestricted access.

The platform should implement role-based access control (RBAC).
Role 1 — Super Administrator
Full system control.
- Manage admin accounts.
- Manage permissions.
- Manage all church records.
- Manage website configuration.
- Access system audit logs.
- Manage database exports.

Role 2 — Church Administrator
Daily church administration.
- Manage members.
- Manage visitors.
- Manage fellowships.
- Manage events.
- Manage updates.
- Manage approved gallery content.

Role 3 — Pastor / Church Leadership
Leadership oversight and pastoral access.
- View permitted member information.
- Review prayer requests according to assigned permissions.
- View visitor follow-up.
- View ministry reports.
- Manage selected church updates or sermons.

Role 4 — Fellowship Leader
Manage an assigned fellowship.
- View their assigned fellowship.
- View authorized members.
- Review joining requests.
- Update fellowship activities.
- Manage fellowship announcements.

Role 5 — Media Administrator
Manage public media and content.
- Upload photographs.
- Manage gallery.
- Manage sermons.
- Manage slideshow.
- Publish approved media.

Role 6 — Prayer Team
Access only assigned prayer requests.
- View assigned requests.
- Update prayer status.
- Record follow-up.
- Mark requests as completed.

Permissions should be configurable
The administrator should be able to assign permissions such as:
- members.view
- members.create
- members.edit
- members.archive
- visitors.view
- visitors.followup
- prayer.view
- prayer.assign
- prayer.update
- events.create
- events.publish
- news.create
- news.publish
- gallery.upload
- gallery.delete
- settings.manage
- admins.manage
Every permission must be checked on the server side. Hiding a button in the frontend is not security.

